 "articoli": {
  "a155": {
   "titolo": "Google faces calls for AI moratoriums in schools after Gemini pilot backlash",
   "link": "https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/11/families-fight-to-keep-googles-disturbing-ai-off-school-issued-chromebooks/",
   "data": "2026-11-12T18:57:45+00:00",
   "testo": "CHICAGO—Families joined protests in seven cities nationwide on Wednesday, rallying against what they see as Google’s bid to entrench its AI models in schools, turn kids of all ages into Gemini guinea pigs, and hook them on AI. The backlash follows a Google statement last week, confirming that there will be no way for students to use Google Search without AI. While Google claimed it was giving schools “more choice, not less”—by turning off Search, Gemini, and Gemini Notebook by default—it would not provide an option that allowed Search without AI. For students, the only way to avoid interacting with AI was to remember to add “-ai” to every query, parents worried. In Chicago, many families showed up to protest Google, despite cold, rainy weather. Waving signs, dozens of protesters marched up to Google’s downtown office as workers seemed to be leaving for the day, shouting chants like “Hey Google, nice try, we don’t want your Gemini” and “Hey, Google, leave our kids alone!” Called to action by a nonprofit grassroots advocacy group, the Illinois Families for Public Schools (ILFPS), some parents and students had previously won a major AI fight. In August, the group pressured Chicago Public Schools (CPS) to pull out of a Gemini pilot that would have roped in all high school students districtwide—without consulting parents. And now, ILFPS is demanding a moratorium on AI until risks to students are better understood and CPS can draft a policy to protect students from potential learning setbacks or more harmful outcomes from engaging Google’s AI bots. If the group wins, it will join cities like Los Angeles and New York City in cautiously banning AI in schools.",
   "fonte": "Ars Technica",
   "tipo": "testata",
   "solo_titolo": false,
   "categoria": "Testate tech",
   "id": "a155",
   "testo_completo": true
  },
  "a92": {
   "titolo": "Introducing Gemini 4 Hydrogen",
   "link": "https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/introducing-gemini-4-hydrogen/",
   "data": "2026-11-12T18:00:00+00:00",
   "testo": "Introducing Gemini 4 Hydrogen Today, we’re introducing Gemini 4 Hydrogen, bringing cutting-edge intelligence to everyone — with speed and efficiency for everyday coding and agentic workflows. While our frontier models, Gemini 4 Carbon and Gemini 4 Argon, excel at deep, long-horizon reasoning on your hardest problems, Hydrogen is built for rapid execution across diverse agentic workflows. With Hydrogen, we’re also bringing Gemini 4 to everyone — for all tiers of Google AI Studio, the Gemini API, Gemini for business, the Gemini app, Google Search, and paid Google AI Pro and Ultra subscribers in Google Antigravity. Hydrogen surpasses Argon on the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index at its default high thinking level (51 vs. 50, and reaching 54 at its highest xhigh thinking setting), while often completing tasks faster than models in its class. Gemini 4 Hydrogen is priced flat across its entire 1 million-token context window at $1.50 per million input tokens and $7.50 per million output tokens. Unlocking efficiencies in multi-model workloads In real-world software development, while teams rely on frontier models like Carbon and Argon for deep architectural judgment when designing systems or untangling subtle bugs, they still need a fast, disciplined execution model for writing code, running tests, and verifying changes. Hydrogen is built to stand on its own as a model for everyday work — or to pair with Carbon and Argon as the high-efficiency execution engine in multi-model workflows. To help make this multi-model collaboration automatic, we’re introducing Orchestrator in Google Antigravity. Rather than using a single model from start to finish, Orchestrator dynamically switches between models like Carbon and Hydrogen within the same session. On Terminal-Bench 4.0, Orch…",
   "fonte": "Google (blog ufficiale)",
   "tipo": "ufficiale",
   "solo_titolo": false,
   "categoria": "Laboratori",
   "id": "a92",
   "testo_completo": true
  },
  "a91": {
   "titolo": "Gemini 4 Hydrogen: everyday coding and agentic workflows for developers",
   "link": "https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/technology/developers-tools/build-with-gemini-4-hydrogen/",
   "data": "2026-11-12T18:00:00+00:00",
   "testo": "Gemini 4 Hydrogen: everyday coding and agentic workflows for developers Starting today, developers can build with Gemini 4 Hydrogen, bringing cutting-edge intelligence to everyone — with speed and efficiency for everyday coding and agentic workflows. Built for rapid iteration, Hydrogen handles repository coding and debugging, interactive web development, and multimodal workflows — running standalone across four configurable thinking levels or alongside frontier models like Gemini 4 Carbon and Gemini 4 Argon. With tighter instruction following and fewer fabricated claims, it helps you ship cleaner, production-ready code faster. Start building with the Gemini API in Google AI Studio and Android Studio, Gemini for business, or, if you are a paid Google AI Pro or Ultra subscriber, try it in Google Antigravity. - Check out the Gemini API What’s New Guide for model details, setup instructions, and thinking-level controls. - Read the Google Antigravity blog to see how new features like Orchestrator pair Hydrogen with Gemini 4 Carbon.",
   "fonte": "Google (blog ufficiale)",
   "tipo": "ufficiale",
